---
import AffLink from '../components/AffLink.astro';
import ProductLink from '../components/ProductLink.astro';
import ProductPhotos from '../components/ProductPhotos.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';
import { batteries } from '../data/site.js';
const pick = ['Anker SOLIX C1000X','Jackery Explorer 2000 v2','EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3','Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus'];
const shots = ['BLUETTI Apex 300','EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3','Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus','Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus'];
const st = pick.map(n => batteries.find(b => b.product === n)).filter(Boolean);
// Champion Power Equipment wattage chart, furnace fan 1/2 HP: 300-800 W running, 1,600 W starting (checked 2026-11-14)
const watts = [300, 550, 800];
const hrs = (b,w) => b.capacity_kwh*1000*0.85/w;
const j2 = st.find(b => b.product==='Jackery Explorer 2000 v2');
const dp3 = st.find(b => b.product==='EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3');
const shot = shots.map(n => batteries.find(b => b.product === n)).filter(Boolean);
const day = 550*12/1000;
const faq = [
["How long will a power station run a gas furnace?", `A 2,042 Wh station runs a typical 550 W furnace blower for about ${hrs(j2,550).toFixed(1)} hours of blower time. If the blower runs 12 hours a day, that is about ${day.toFixed(1)} kWh per day, so one 2 kWh station covers well under one day.`],
["Why does a gas furnace need electricity?", "The blower fan, control board, and igniter run on electricity, so most modern gas furnaces will not heat when the power is out, even though the fuel is gas."],
["How many watts does a furnace blower use?", "The Champion wattage chart lists 300–800 W running and up to 1,600 W to start for a 1/2 HP furnace fan. Your furnace's label or manual has the exact figure."],
["Can I just plug my furnace into a power station?", "Only if it is set up for it. Most furnaces are hardwired, so the safe routes are a listed transfer switch or interlock installed by a licensed electrician, or a plug-in furnace circuit. Never backfeed through an outlet."],
["Is it safe to run a station indoors during a winter outage?", "A battery station makes no exhaust, unlike a generator. Never run a gas generator indoors or near a window. See our generator safety page for the CDC and Ready.gov rules."]
];
---
<Base title="How Long Will a Power Station Run a Gas Furnace?" description="A gas furnace blower uses 300-800 W and up to 1,600 W to start. Runtime on a 2 kWh power station, daily kWh, and how to connect it safely." schema={[{ '@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(([q,a]) => ({ '@type':'Question', name:q, acceptedAnswer:{ '@type':'Answer', text:a } })) }]}>
<h1>How long will a power station run a gas furnace?</h1>
<p class="lede"><strong>Short answer:</strong> about {hrs(j2,550).toFixed(0)} hours of blower time on a 2,000 Wh station, which is less than a day at 12 hours of blower run time. A gas furnace only needs the blower, controls, and igniter, about 550 W on average, but it still adds up to roughly {day.toFixed(1)} kWh a day. Figures as of 2026-11-14.</p>
<p class="note">Your furnace's label (watts, or amps × 120 V) beats any number below.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Furnace watts</h2>
<p>A gas furnace burns gas for heat, but the blower fan, control board, and igniter need electricity. The <a href="https://www.championpowerequipment.com/generator-wattage-chart/">Champion Power Equipment wattage chart</a> lists a 1/2 HP furnace fan at <strong>300–800 W running</strong> and <strong>1,600 W starting</strong> (checked 2026-11-14). We use 550 W, the midpoint, as the example.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Runtime math</h2>
<p><strong>Hours = battery Wh × 0.85 ÷ furnace watts.</strong> <span class="badge est">Assumption: 85% usable. Hours are blower-on hours, not clock hours</span></p>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Station (capacity, output, surge)</th>{watts.map(w => <th>{w} W blower</th>)}</tr></thead><tbody>
{st.map(b => <tr><td><ProductLink product={b.product} button={false} /> ({(b.capacity_kwh*1000).toLocaleString()} Wh, {b.output_w.toLocaleString()} W, {b.surge_w.toLocaleString()} W surge)<br/><AffLink product={b.product} /></td>{watts.map(w => <td>≈ {hrs(b,w).toFixed(1)} h</td>)}</tr>)}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="src">Capacities and ratings from our <a href="/portable-power-stations/">power station table</a> (November 16, 2026). Example: {(j2.capacity_kwh*1000).toLocaleString()} Wh × 0.85 ÷ 550 W = {hrs(j2,550).toFixed(1)} hours.</p>

<h2>Step 3: A day and a night</h2>
<p><span class="badge est">Estimate</span> If the blower runs 12 hours a day, 550 W × 12 h = <strong>{day.toFixed(1)} kWh per day</strong>. After the 0.85 usable factor that is {(day/0.85).toFixed(1)} kWh of battery per day. The {dp3.product} ({(dp3.capacity_kwh*1000).toLocaleString()} Wh) covers about {(dp3.capacity_kwh*0.85/day).toFixed(1)} days of that, a 2 kWh station about {(j2.capacity_kwh*0.85/day).toFixed(1)} days. Run the blower fewer hours, and it stretches.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Connect it safely</h2>
<p>Most furnaces are hardwired. The safe options are a listed transfer switch or interlock kit installed by a licensed electrician, or a furnace circuit built for a plug-in. The station's output must also cover the 1,600 W start on the Champion chart: the 1,800 W C1000X is just above it, and larger units have more headroom. <a href="/transfer-switch-vs-interlock-kit/">Transfer switch vs interlock →</a></p>
<p>For longer winter outages see <a href="/winter-storm-power-outage-heat/">keeping the heat on for 3 days</a>, solar recharging in <a href="/solar-backup/">solar backup</a>, or <a href="/power-station-vs-generator-cost/">power station vs generator cost</a>. A plug-in space heater is a worse use of stored energy (<a href="/how-long-will-a-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">space heater runtime</a>).</p>
<p><a href="/calculator/">Add your furnace and essentials in the calculator →</a> · <a href="/what-size-battery-generator/">Sizing guide</a></p>

<h2>Picks that can start the blower</h2>
<p>These four stations are the ones we have press photos for. Each lists continuous output above the 800 W running high end and surge or lifting power above the 1,600 W start on the Champion chart. Hours below are blower-on time at 550 W, using 85% of rated watt-hours. A gas generator has to stay outdoors: Ready.gov says at least 20 feet from windows, doors, and attached garages (<a href="/generator-safety/">generator safety</a>). A battery makes no exhaust, so it can sit inside by the transfer equipment.</p>
<ProductPhotos products={shots} title="Stations with press photos" />
{shot.map(b => <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:12px">
<h3><ProductLink product={b.product} /></h3>
<p>At 550 W this is about <strong>{hrs(b,550).toFixed(1)} hours</strong> of blower time ({(b.capacity_kwh*1000).toLocaleString()} Wh × 0.85 ÷ 550). Output is {b.output_w.toLocaleString()} W{b.product==='EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3' ? ' (up to 6,000 W with X-Boost)' : b.product==='BLUETTI Apex 300' ? ', with 7,680 W lifting power' : `, ${b.surge_w.toLocaleString()} W surge`}.</p>
{b.product==='BLUETTI Apex 300' && <p>BLUETTI lists generator charging up to 11.5 kW at 120/240 V. Solar input is listed up to 4,000 W, and the page says that figure requires a SolarX 4K. The page chrome says "Global Warranty"; the spec text we read on 2026-11-14 did not state a term in years. Sale price is $1,499 ($1,699 before the sale). The page says the unit is expected to ship in late January. It beats a gas generator here because the blower runs for hours inside the house, with no fuel cans and no exhaust to keep 20 feet from the windows.</p>}
{b.product==='EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3' && <p>EcoFlow lists 2,600 W maximum solar input (two PV ports) and says solar, car, or outlet charging reaches 80% in 50 minutes. AC charging input is listed as 1,800 W at 100–120 V. The spec block lists a 5-year warranty (3-year standard plus a 2-year extended warranty when you register). Sale price is $2,799 ($3,299 regular); the standalone unit was unavailable on 2026-11-14. It also has 120/240 V output, so the same station can cover other 240 V loads the blower does not need. Indoors, with no engine, it avoids the carbon monoxide problem a generator brings to a winter outage.</p>}
{b.product==='Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus' && <p>Anker lists up to 3,200 W solar input and says the station fully recharges in under 2 hours in optimal sunlight. The same page says 240 V AC wall outlets can recharge it faster, without a wall-charge hour count. It lists a 5-year warranty and a 10-year lifespan claim. The price on the page is $2,399.99 with code WSTD26BFC6 ($600 off $2,999.99). The unit was sold out on 2026-11-14. Six thousand watts of output leaves headroom if the blower starts while something else is on, which a small generator sized only for the fan does not.</p>}
{b.product==='Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus' && <p>Jackery says it recharges in about 2 hours with up to 4,000 W of solar input. A footnote says a separate 1.7-hour charge time was measured through the Smart Transfer Switch, not a wall outlet. The page lists a 5-year warranty when you buy from Jackery.com, and says "11 years" is theoretical cell life from 4,000 cycles. Standalone price is $3,499 and was unavailable; the page says pre-orders ship Nov 28. The bundle with a battery pack was $5,899 ($6,199 list) and in stock. At 5,040 Wh it is the longest blower runtime of these four, still with no gasoline and no exhaust in the house.</p>}
</div>)}
<h2>FAQ</h2>
{faq.map(([q,a]) => <details class="card" style="margin-bottom:10px"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}
<Newsletter />
</Base>
